  prose <<~PROSE
    The ring built its fragments tall, and its own lifts died with the Glassfall. The balance is what the Rekindling rigged through the corpses: a salvaged head-wheel at the shaft crown, one certified line, two open cars balanced against each other, and a brake a child can throw. The engineering is deliberately plain — every part visible, every part local — and the plainness is the point, because a balance is maintained by the neighborhood that rides it, on a rota posted at the gate like the rated load.

    Balance does the work. Passengers going down wind ballast up; freight going up rides the water-cans down; and the winder's whole craft is loading the paired cars so the wheel wants to turn in the direction of the day's traffic. A practiced loader moves a shift's worth of people and goods on arm strength and arithmetic, running the pair car-for-car through the rhythm the shaft's regulars know by feel — the pause at the gates, the counterweight's sigh, the brake's bite at the landing. Long fragments ladder their height in stages, balance handing to balance at gallery landings that grew into markets, because a landing is where everyone waits and a wait is a customer.
  PROSE

  prose <<~PROSE, section: :operations, heading: "The Shaft Community"
    A shaft is a commons and runs like one. The gate rota covers winding, brake watch, and line inspection; the rated load is painted fresh each year at the shaft festival, and weighing the ambiguous load is gate ceremony — the scale hangs beside the gate, and the loader's ruling stands. Balance privileges are the hab's small politics: the shift crews ride first at the turn, the water-cans hold standing bookings on the down car, and a household moving furniture negotiates with the loader in advance, usually in baking.

    Shaft songs pace the winding where crews wind by hand, and the treadwheel rigs — freight balances walked by teams inside the wheel — keep the oldest work music in the habs. Riders on the passenger car hear the wheel crew's verse rising past them through the shaft, and answering the verse from the car is balance courtesy of the settled kind: the voice that answers is a neighbor, and the shaft knows its own.
  PROSE

  cue "Two open cars pass each other mid-shaft with a rope's sigh, the descending passengers nodding to the ascending water-cans, and somewhere above the head-wheel turns on grease the rota renewed last week."
  cue "At the gate a loader weighs a crate on the hanging scale, chalks the number on the car frame, and shifts one water-can to the other car with the air of a judge issuing a ruling."
  affordance "A balance moves a neighborhood's people and freight up a dead fragment on arm strength and counterweight, and its gate — with the scale, the rota, and the loader's standing ruling — is the shaft community's working parliament."
  pressure "The machine is a balance, so every load is everyone's business: an off-manifest crate arrives at the bottom fast, and the gate ceremony that prevents it runs on neighbors enforcing weight on neighbors."
  variation "Neighborhood pairs run on hand winding and favors; the great treadwheel freight rigs walk to shift-long work songs and hire their wheel crews like boat crews."
  variation "Compound shafts hand traffic balance to balance at gallery landings that grew into markets, and a landing's stall rents track the balance schedule the way harbor rents track the tide."
end
